## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- MinHash sketches for Jaccard similarity
- Batch processing costs vs per-element costs
- Trade-offs: d (accuracy) vs memory, impact of collisions
- Exact vs approximate counting implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: initialize sketches for A and B
class MinHash {
  constructor(d, maxHash) { this.d = d; this.sig = Array(d).fill(Infinity); this.maxHash = maxHash; }
  add(x) { for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) { const h = hash_i(x, i) % this.maxHash; if (h < this.sig[i]) this.sig[i] = h; } }
  resembles(other) { let matches = 0; for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) if (this.sig[i] === other.sig[i]) matches++; return matches / this.d; }
}
```

